WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.660
There's more to Keanu Reeves than the characters
he plays, and he's got the fascinating life

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:05.380
story to prove it.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.740
Here's a look at just how the John Wick star
went from a high school dropout to the captivating

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.440
actor he is today.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:16.180
Reeves is Canadian, but he was actually born
in Beirut, Lebanon, where his mother, Patricia

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.840
Taylor, a British costume designer and performer,
met his father, Samuel Reeves.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.700
But his dad was essentially out of the picture
by the time Reeves was a toddler.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.320
As a result, he basically went wherever his
mom's career took them.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.060
They lived in Sydney and New York City, and
eventually, they settled down in Toronto.

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:33.980
Reeves definitely has a multicultural background.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.800
In addition to his travels, he was also exposed
to Chinese and Hawaiian culture through his

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.300
grandmother.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.900
He has also said that his mom taught him British
mannerisms and passed down some of those customs

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:44.700
and formal attitudes.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.860
His upbringing may have been unconventional,
but he definitely became more cultured because

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:49.360
of it.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.320
Reeves' mother was quite well-connected in
her line of work.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.540
As a result, little Keanu met lots of celebrities
when he was a kid.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.390
His mother worked for David Bowie, and he
also remembers spending time with Emmylou

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.420
Harris and Dolly Parton.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.180
Apparently, Alice Cooper even babysat for
him.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.840
"I remember that he, uh, that there was fake
poo in the fridge."

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.080
That was apparently par for the course.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.680
"That's like an Alice Cooper bit?"

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.259
“That’s pretty funny that he did that.”

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.340
“The next time we talk to Alice, we’ll
say, ‘Do you remember doing that?’”

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.200
"Oh, I'm sure he remembers that."

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.120
It's easy to see why Reeves became interested
in acting at a young age, as an early exposure

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.360
to so many celebrities surely made it seem
like breaking into Hollywood wasn't a far-fetched

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:30.860
fantasy.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.280
If his mother could work her way up and connect
with some of the biggest rock stars in the

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.800
world, why couldn't he achieve his own dreams?

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.540
Reeves is an exceptionally talented actor,
but he was not the world's best student.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.980
Over the course of five years, he attended
several different high schools.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.140
When he was a freshman, he wanted to go to
a school that was highly ranked for academics,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.940
but later, he decided to audition for Toronto's
Etobicoke School of the Arts.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.520
He was accepted, but unfortunately, he ended
up getting expelled.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.539
He's said that he enjoyed his time there,
but he's also admitted that he simply wasn't

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.760
cut out for such a structured academic environment.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.780
Reeves isn't the only actor in Hollywood who
didn't finish high school, and like many other

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.820
creative young people, he thrived in alternative
environments.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.700
He also had dyslexia, which certainly didn't
help matters.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:17.260
But he enrolled in a class called "Respect
for Acting," as well as sketch and improv

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:21.700
courses with Second City Toronto, and he continued
attending these classes at night and honing

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:22.200
his craft.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.380
Reeves is a man of many talents.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.740
In addition to acting in movies and theater,
he's also spent plenty of time on the ice

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:29.340
playing hockey.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.560
In fact, at one point in his life, he hoped
to play professionally.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.240
While attending De La Salle College in Toronto,
he played goalie, and he was quite the formidable

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:37.740
opponent.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.740
He was so skilled at netminding that he earned
the nickname "The Wall."

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.460
When he was younger, Reeves dreamed about
representing Canada in the Olympics one day.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.200
But when he decided to dedicate himself to
acting, he put that dream on the back burner.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.560
He continued to play hockey on a recreational
basis, but he chose to put acting first.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.300
Reeves may have started out as a young jack
of all trades, but he chose to commit his

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.360
life to becoming a master of one, and that
decision has clearly paid off.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.480
One of Reeves' first professional gigs was
working as a correspondent on the Canadian

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.200
Broadcasting Corporation youth program Going
Great.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.980
The show focused on child prodigies who were
able to accomplish amazing things at a young

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:13.480
age.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.080
"And how long did you do it?"

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.040
"Umm, they fired me after the first year."

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.800
"They got rid of you!"

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.040
"I got one year of Going Great!"

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.590
Reeves also appeared in another local TV production
called Hangin' In, a scripted series about

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:30.220
kids who attend a counseling center in a rough
part of Toronto.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.200
He described Hangin' In as a "godsend" for
young actors in Toronto at the time who were

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.360
just trying to get a foot in the door and
make some connections in the industry.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.840
Reeves is not just skilled as an actor, he's
also a gifted musician, and he was the bassist

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.620
in a band called Dogstar in the '90s and 2000s.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.040
The band got its start when Reeves ran into
vocalist Robert Mailhouse in a grocery store

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:50.540
in 1991.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.680
The two bonded over their mutual love of hockey,
and eventually, they realized that they both

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.020
liked jamming out together, too.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.120
Two more friends joined the band, and as their
fanbase grew, they started touring.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.720
They even played some pretty big festivals,
like Glastonbury in England.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.640
The band broke up after releasing their second
album because the members were getting busy

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:09.340
with other work commitments.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.100
In particular, Reeves' career was really taking
off with the popularity of the Matrix films.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.720
But this wasn't the end of Reeves' musicianship,
as he and Mailhouse would go on to perform

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.180
together some more in a band called Becky.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.960
Maybe he can whip out that bass in the next
John Wick movie.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.040
Shredding on some strings could totally be
a useful tool in an assassin's arsenal.

